(TIM-36 - `in <unit>` casts a duration to that unit, and the two units
 agree with each other: a 1500-millisecond job reads 1500 in milliseconds
 and 1 in seconds, so the seconds cast truncates rather than rounding.
 The singular spellings and the `ms` alias take the same position.
 Also covers: TIM-37, TIM-38, TIM-39.
 Ran: VOX_CORE_PATH=/home/josj/scr/english/vox/coreasm /home/josj/scr/english/vox/target/release/vox TIM-36.vox -o p && ./p
 expected output:
   the millisecond cast reports the whole 1500
   the second cast truncates 1.5 seconds to 1
   millisecond and milliseconds are the same cast
   ms and milliseconds are the same cast
   elapsed takes the same cast as duration
)
a timer called 'job timer'.
Start the 'job timer'.
Wait 1500 milliseconds.
Stop the 'job timer'.
a number called 'in milliseconds' is the 'job timer''s duration in milliseconds.
a number called 'in seconds' is the 'job timer''s duration in seconds.
a number called 'in the singular' is the 'job timer''s duration in millisecond.
a number called 'in the alias' is the 'job timer''s duration in ms.
a number called 'elapsed in seconds' is the 'job timer''s elapsed in seconds.
If 'in milliseconds' is greater than 1499 and 'in milliseconds' is less than 2500 then, print "the millisecond cast reports the whole 1500".
Otherwise, print "the millisecond cast reports {'in milliseconds'}".
If 'in seconds' is 1 then, print "the second cast truncates 1.5 seconds to 1".
Otherwise, print "the second cast reports {'in seconds'}".
If 'in the singular' is 'in milliseconds' then, print "millisecond and milliseconds are the same cast".
Otherwise, print "the singular cast reports {'in the singular'}".
If 'in the alias' is 'in milliseconds' then, print "ms and milliseconds are the same cast".
Otherwise, print "the ms cast reports {'in the alias'}".
If 'elapsed in seconds' is 'in seconds' then, print "elapsed takes the same cast as duration".
Otherwise, print "elapsed in seconds reports {'elapsed in seconds'}".
